@@ -7,6 +7,8 @@
 3.19 (2021-11-20)
 -----------------
 
+- ``sqlite-utils insert`` now has a repeatable ``--extract`` option for
+  extracting columns into separate lookup tables while inserting rows.
 - The :ref:`table.lookup() method <python_api_lookup_tables>` now accepts keyword arguments that match those on the underlying ``table.insert()`` method: ``foreign_keys=``, ``column_order=``, ``not_null=``, ``defaults=``, ``extracts=``, ``conversions=`` and ``columns=``. You can also now pass ``pk=`` to specify a different column name to use for the primary key. (:issue:`342`)
 
 .. _v3_18:
diff --git a/docs/cli.rst b/docs/cli.rst
index a096fdeaa7887dacb1a4d37aa15f76c4236763e6..b008b45109d4833d18db4732b0927776f13f6a22 100644
--- a/docs/cli.rst
+++ b/docs/cli.rst
@@ -712,6 +712,15 @@ You can delete all the existing rows in the table before inserting the new recor
 
     $ sqlite-utils insert dogs.db dogs dogs.json --truncate
 
+You can extract repeated values in a column into a separate lookup table using
+``--extract``::
+
+    $ sqlite-utils insert trees.db trees trees.json --extract species
+
+This replaces values in the ``species`` column with integer foreign keys to a
+new ``species`` table. Use ``--extract`` more than once to extract multiple
+columns.
+
 .. _cli_inserting_data_binary:
 
 Inserting binary data
